      "label": "Trap sallow · success",
      "data": {
        "condition": "The player tries to catch Sallow out in his own words in front of the King: pressing him where his story doesn't hold ('condemned or lost to goblins, which is it?'), confronting him with what was seen and said (the lavender, the wheat-ear ring, the girl paid a crown, Hobb's word), or leading him into a lie the King can catch, where it could go either way. The roll follows how they go about it: an argument, a bluff, or menace. (Comparing his papers is the other way, not this; getting his arrangements wrong to his face, to see him correct them, needs no dice.)",
        "phase": "attempt",
        "skill": "persuasion",
        "dc": 13,
        "scope": "beat",
        "branch": "success",
        "outcomeText": "Sallow answers too quickly, and too well: he corrects the record on something he couldn't know if he were innocent, in detail, in front of the King, and hears himself a moment too late. The King has heard it too. He puts his cup down.",
